Date: Fri, 12 Feb 1993 11:25:02 -0800 (PST)
From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: KIDS: well.... kids

On 12 Feb 1993, Duncan & Lois Frissell wrote:

> We have 4 kids (currently members of 'Generation X' but making more dough
> than those whiners) who attended every sort of school.  Their maturation
> corresponded with my wife's radicalization (I did not meet them until they
> were 7-14 years of age).  Through lack of knowledge, they spent some time 
> in slave schools but were we doing it again we would employ deadly force
> if necessary to prevent that occurrence.
> 
> Slave schools vary in quality but the one thing (the only thing) they cannot
> avoid teaching is state supremacy.

Well, let me take a little exception here.  Just because something is
taught doesn't mean you have to learn it.

My kids all go to public schools, for no better reason than that my wife
and I weren't willing (should I euphemistically say, "didn't consider
ourselves realistically able", or something?) to commit to the investment
of time and energy necessary to home school properly, and couldn't afford
private alternatives.  Now, I'm certainly not about to shill for the academic
quality of public schooling--my kids all found themselves knowing a lot more than their
teachers (both in terms of specific information and in general terms of
how to think and reason) from quite early on, and in a grim sort of
way this is quite educational.

But as to teaching state supremacy, there's no reason at all that kids
have to be negatively affected by that crap, as long as they can get a
voice of reason at home.  In fact, I think (though I have direct
experience only one way) that it may be easier to teach necessary
anti-statist, anti-collectivist lessons to kids who are regularly
encountering the benevolent hand of state control in their daily lives,
than to kids who are insulated from any direct experience of it.

My oldest boy (9th grade) is a fervent libertarian (I should probably say,
"raving anarchist"--he's still young and excitable)--as, by the way, are a
number of his schoolmates.  The public schools have utterly failed (to
their evident and sometimes vocal distress) to teach him respect for
authority, and every time they try--and every time he sees them succeed in
teaching his peers--it simply confirms for him the lessons I have tried to
teach him at home.

And this was pretty much my own experience of slave schools as well.  They
gave me an inferior academic education, which my parents, and I myself,
had to do a lot to supplement.  But they gave me one hell of a good
political education, and I can't see any other way I could have gotten
that at that age.

Scott DeLancey



