Date: Fri, 12 Feb 93 14:47:48 EST
From: sulko-m@acsu.buffalo.edu (Mark A. Sulkowski)
Subject: PHIL: Self-Transformation and "genetic relativism"

Joseph Truitt writes:
>However, some of those same people feel that artificially changing
>abilities, lifespan, apparent age, race, or sex might be somehow less
>ethical--maybe even disrespectful to our creator(s):
>
>Somehow, that second category of transformation invades a sacred space--
>like the body shouldn't be messed with TOO much.  Don't cheat!
>
>Assuming self-motivation and full consentuality, why should it be
>ethically different to make one kind of body modification vs. another?  Is
>there really a sacred line we should avoid crossing, lest we loose a
>valuable part of our individual self, or [goddess forbid] our humanity?

	I'm not exactly sure where people get this notion.  I think it is
a (highly popular) remnant of pre-Darwin thinking.  People have a concept
of the ideal human which fits some kind of special mold (from God?) to
which we should all aspire and esteem.  This ideal human is heterosexual
(of course), has no genetic defects, a limited life span, and is completely
a product of his/her ideal, pre-established nature.

	Our knowledge of evolution is a threat to this paradigm.  Evolutionists
must recognize that there is no such thing as the ideal human.  There is no
special mold, no ideal, no such thing as a single, perfect human nature.
There is no genetically perfect representative of the human race.  This
leaves the door open to thoughts of self-transformation.  After all, if we
aspire to be more than human, why is that a bad thing?  If there is no
perfect human nature, then perhaps transhuman nature is better.  Evolution
got us where we are.  Why not accelerate the process?

	Interestingly, naturalistic moralities tend to tacitly assume that
human nature is immutable.  Aristotelianism is an early example; Objectivism
is a recent example.  Such philosophies hold that desires should be limited
by reason through a conceptual understanding of what is good based on human
nature.  A philosophy like extropianism, which seeks to fulfill as many desires
as possible through technology and particularly self-transformation, turns 
this kind of thinking on its head.  The Good is no longer limited to what is
good for human beings -- for we may have the ability to become more than human!
Desires which may be inappropriate (or immoral) for a human being may be
quite moral and good for a different kind of being.  All we have to do is
figure out how to transform ourselves into the kind of being that will fulfill
our desires.  So, desire is no longer the slave of reason.  It is the other
way around.

	BTW, do not construe from this that I am no longer a moral
objectivist (small-o).  I do believe that there is a good and bad
for living beings, but I do not assume that one's own genetic nature
is the greatest good out of all genetic natures.  I am not a genetic
"relativist".

