Subject: VR: Topologies of virtual worlds

D Anton Sherwood writes:
> First, it must satisfy the sense(s) by which its users navigate.  For us,
> that means sight. 

Which brings up a very interesting point.  Why not design systems that
allow for more than one virtual reality instantiation?  This in fact
seems to be the direction implicit in the current X-windows technology,
where the program defines windows but it is up to the window manager how
those windows get instantiated (program X starts iconified, program Y's
main window takes up half the screen when it starts, etc, etc).

If VR objects can define properties or behaviours through well defined
shared protocols, it should be possible for there to be an unlimited
number of 'realizations' of the VR, potentialy one for each person
interfacing with the VR.  I would expect there to be commonly used
realizations, probably with relatively small degrees of presonal
customization (I prefer keyboard focus, Jane prefers pointer focus, I
like 2 pixel wide window boarders, Jane prefers 5 pixels; that sort of
thing).  But there could also be coextant realizations with major
differences.

For example, the visual one, and then the one for those who are blind . . . 

-- 
